await writeFile(L27+'notes.md',`l27 starts from l25. Only its global optical-depth multiplier changes, to ${factor}; the effective strength is ${num(result.dustStrength)} = 1.2 × ${factor}. Dust shapes, relative column contrast, lane coordinates, depth ordering and stellar light remain exact. There is no clumping, smoothing or color retuning.\n\n| Fixed lane pixels / adjacent bar | Dimming | R/B color contrast | Lane overlap with photo |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n${row('Horálek',photo,1)}\n${row('l25',rec(before.measurement),num(priorLocation.intersectionOverUnion))}\n${row('l26',l26m,num(rec(l26q.l26VersusPhoto).intersectionOverUnion))}\n${row('l27',measurement,num(location.intersectionOverUnion))}\n\nAcceptance requires dimming within 1 percentage point, color contrast within 10%, and overlap at least ${num(priorLocation.intersectionOverUnion).toFixed(6)}. The l27 errors are ${errors.dimmingPercentagePoints.toFixed(2)} percentage points and ${errors.colorContrastPercent.toFixed(2)}% in color contrast. Whole-bar R/G and B/G errors are ${errors.barColorPercent.map(v=>v.toFixed(2)+'%').join(' and ')}. All check results are saved in acceptance.json.\n\nThe native scalar curve and the unchanged zero-dust reference are recorded. Even with internal dust disabled, these same model lane positions are ${(100*num(rec(control.clear).depth)).toFixed(2)}% dimmer than adjacent model bar light, while the photo is ${(100*num(photo.depth)).toFixed(2)}% dimmer. Thus the statistic includes the fixed stellar brightness pattern; it does not measure dust alone. No stellar light or measurement masks were changed to hide this limitation.\n\nTraining and held-out RMS are ${num(residuals.trainRelativeRms).toFixed(6)} and ${num(residuals.heldoutRelativeRms).toFixed(6)}. RMS is not the gate. Side views remain coherent. ${acceptance.status}\n`);
await writeFile(L27+'method.md',`The exact accepted l25 finite dust bank is reused. Its 86,690 HERITAGE samples retain their five positive depth samples, every three-dimensional position, width and weight, and their adopted 250 pc Gaussian depth distribution. OGLE regional foreground ordering is untouched. The sole free parameter is k within 0.01–1, applied through the existing dust strength as 1.2k, so tau_lambda,l27 = k tau_lambda,l25 everywhere. The native spatial profile and its normalized contrast are unchanged. Optical depth, not source mass or cloud geometry, is calibrated. The original Gordon broad optical channel ratios and Milky Way foreground remain fixed.\n\nThe dimming and color measurements use the exact l26 fixed l25 lane mask and nearby-bar definition, with the same reference registration. No lane pixels are reselected for the candidate. Color contrast is (R/B inside lanes)/(R/B adjacent bar), equivalently the paired R/G ratio divided by the paired B/G ratio. The native image has 1944 fixed lane pixels. Color ratios use displayed RGB; dimming uses decoded linear green. The overlap check separately detects brown, dark structures in both images using the unmodified l25 diagnostic. Its full-precision l25 overlap is the minimum, rather than a rounded 0.201.\n\nTiny and native calibration use the same canonical renderer and code. A multiplier of one must reproduce l25 photons on all rendered support. The fast bar mask includes both the bar aperture and every fractional valid boundary pixel used by the local-light statistic; this matters at tiny size. An initial tiny mask that omitted fractional boundary pixels was corrected before native calibration. Its superseded local record is preserved. The final full bar is rendered without a mask and must replay the native calibration exactly.\n\nThe one-dimensional fit minimizes lane-dimming error on a declared four-point native grid. Color contrast, lane overlap and whole-bar color are independent acceptance checks. They are not used to select the unchanged parent instead of fitting the requested dimming. A positive lower bound retains dust; the zero-dust case is only a reused diagnostic reference. It demonstrates intrinsic stellar brightness differences at the lane locations, not an alternative measured dust geometry. No per-pixel transfer, dust-color parameter or modification to unresolved stellar emission is introduced.\n`);
